A||||8||jeffndebrus||red glowing manifolds? fire ?||||1||
Z||000000||jeffndebrus||08-30-2001||12:09 AM||||just started my 85 2.8v6 tonight after dark and noticed after only a minute that the manifolds or headers were glowing red hot <BR>?is this normal?  no the belts were off and the water pump was not turning \, however I only ran it for sixty seconds.<BR>:update I am still looking at the aurora engines that I found last week but desperately need to know what kind of money it will take to have someone do the conversion for me.  Ace engineering and archies both ignored my e-mail??||||reg||1
Z||000001||Smoooooth GT||08-30-2001||01:04 AM||||I might not have your answer... but , I do know that a good vacuummmm  leak will cause that...  Mine did same thing awhile back.. I located the leak..  no more *glowing* manifold's.<BR>do a search for *glowing manifold's*..  good luck..   Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||08-30-2001||01:09 AM||||Sounds about right, they get hot pretty fast.<BR>You should see what mine look like at night with the cracked manifolds. <P>About the Aurora engine. Archie only does small block chevy swaps and in most cases wouldn't do the olds swap for you. (Anything's possible though)<BR>I don't know why ACE hasn't gotten back with you. If you are going to have a shop do the swap for you, then you are probably going to need an Aurora transmission, wiring haness and computer also (possibly an aftermarket one). If you can't find those, then either buy the motors and sell them to try and make a few bucks, or you should probably just forget about the crate motors and start looking for a donor car with a complete drivetrain. If you're going to do that, you might as well look for a 4.6 Northstar.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-30-2001||07:16 AM||||most Ive seen have a little glow in dark, but if its very obvious you have a vacume leak, more air makes it run leaner/hotter than normal. If you let it go, the valves and pistons will do the same thing (burn thru).||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||08-30-2001||08:59 AM||||Could also be a clogged catatonic converter.  Mine did that when it plugged up.  Is the engine running hot?  That would be one thing to check...||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||08-30-2001||10:46 AM||||Jeff,  Don't depend on email for getting quotes or ordering parts.  Most of the time the vendors want to have a conversation to get your requirements and expectations down in one interaction.  If you try to do that by email, it will take days if they have extra time or weeks or months if they don't.  Best to look up the number, pick up the phone and talk with them when they have a few minutes (yes they will call you back).  Write all of your questions out on paper 1st so you don't forget anything.  You will have a fairly unique Fiero as I don't think there are many Olds powered Fieros out and about.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||08-30-2001||02:51 PM||||There's place on Blanding Blvd that builds a lot of wild imports, called Speed Tech (or something like that), I think.  I've heard stories (from the Honda guys) of them building a couple of wild Fieros (V-8's, turbos) also.  You may want to give them a try for the swap.<BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, and welcome to the board.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||HummelHund||08-30-2001||05:08 PM||||I'd also do a quick timing check. If it's far enough off it can cause glowing red manifolds.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||7
Z||000008||Archie||08-30-2001||09:14 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Jeff,  Don't depend on email for getting quotes or ordering parts.  Most of the time the vendors want to have a conversation to get your requirements and expectations down in one interaction.  If you try to do that by email, it will take days if they have extra time or weeks or months if they don't.  Best to look up the number, pick up the phone and talk with them when they have a few minutes (yes they will call you back).  Write all of your questions out on paper 1st so you don't forget anything.  You will have a fairly unique Fiero as I don't think there are many Olds powered Fieros out and about.  GL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is some of the best advice ever passed out on the Forum. <P>It is very hard and time consuming to type up a quote based on an email inquiry. 99% of the time the person requesting the quote doesn't supply enough info to have an accurate quote. The person requesting the quote many times doesn't know that there are many variations and options that can effect the quoted price. I can do a quote on the telephone in 10 minutes because I know what questions I need answered. The same quote can take several days and a couple hours of typing just to get close.<P>The only engine swaps I do are the Small Block Chevy. I don't do 3.1's, 3.4's, 3.8's, Aurora's, NStars, little Caddies, etal.<P>I am convinced that the SBC is the best swap for the Fiero. I feel that it is the PERFECT engine swap for any and all Fieros. IMHO, the SBC has a wider range of power outputs & configurations. It also provides the best Cost/performance/reliability ratio.<P>Some other vendors will sell you whatever you want to buy. They will sell you 3.1's, 3.4's, 3.8's, Aurora's, NStars, little Caddies or whatever you think you want. They may know a little about each one of those swaps but they are an "Expert" at none of them. I will only sell you something that I would put in my own car myself, & that's the SBC swap. I will only sell you something that I will drive myself.<P>Let me give you an example. Several years ago (1991) I designed and assembled a kit for the Grand National engine into a Fiero. After building a couple of them, I decided that for a cost/performance/reliability ratio, the SBC was a better swap. Today, I have 48 SBC swap kits in stock and zero (-0-) GN kits in stock. Even though I can & have built a few GN powered Fieros, I don't inventory kits for that swap because I feel that the SBC swap is a smarter choice.<P>Let me make one thing perfectly clear, I'm not trying to flame the "Other Vendors" they are free to sell whatever they want. They probally make much more money than I do since they try to sell to everyone (& that's what a good businessman should do). I personally just can't get excited about selling you something that I'm not sold on myself.<P>I hope this all makes sense.<P>Archie<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
